# Line endings. Sam's ruling 7, 2026-09-06.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. This repo is worked from a Windows clone and from Linux cloud
# containers at the same time. With no rule here, Git stores whatever each
# machine writes, so the same file can come back with every line changed and a
# diff that is 100% noise — which is exactly what a 46-file changes list on a
# Windows checkout turned out to be on 2026-09-06.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE ADDING IT: 2,486 tracked text files are in scope, and exactly
# ONE was committed with Windows line endings. So this normalizes almost nothing
# today and prevents the whole class from here on. It is cheap now precisely
# because it is early.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git decide what is text and store it with LF, while checking
# it out however the platform wants. Binary files are listed explicitly rather
# than left to the heuristic: a .docx or .xlsx corrupted by newline translation
# fails silently and late.

*                 text=auto

# Always LF, whatever the platform — these are read by tooling that cares.
*.sh              text eol=lf
*.py              text eol=lf
*.yml             text eol=lf
*.yaml            text eol=lf

# Windows-native, always CRLF.
*.ps1             text eol=crlf
*.bat             text eol=crlf
*.cmd             text eol=crlf

# Never touched. Newline translation silently corrupts every one of these.
*.png             binary
*.jpg             binary
*.jpeg            binary
*.gif             binary
*.ico             binary
*.webp            binary
*.pdf             binary
*.docx            binary
*.xlsx            binary
*.pptx            binary
*.zip             binary
*.woff            binary
*.woff2           binary
*.ttf             binary
*.otf             binary
*.mp4             binary
*.mov             binary
